<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaghab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/933">933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Umm al-Muqtadir, al-Sayyida, Na'ima</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaghab was the mother of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Muqtadir (r. 908–932) and one of the most powerful figures of his reign. Reportedly of Greek origin, she was a slave who became a concubine of Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> and was freed after bearing al-Muqtadir in 895. When her thirteen-year-old son became the first underage caliph in Muslim history, she sat on his regency council, according to al-Tanukhi, and ran a parallel bureaucracy from the harem, influencing the appointment and dismissal of officials through stewardesses such as Umm Musa and Thumal. Later historians usually portrayed her as a rapacious schemer; she also spent large sums on public charity.</p></div>
